  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.653,
            0.046,
            0.862,
            0.065
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Proportional Reasoning-1"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.291,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.2 Ratios"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.123,
            0.847,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We use the notion of a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ratio",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to represent such proportional relationships in mathematics."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.16,
            0.728,
            0.18
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can say that the ratio of width to height of image A is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.461,
            0.183,
            0.537,
            0.203
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_4.jpg",
          "content": "6 0 : 4 0 ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.208,
            0.704,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The numbers 60 and 40 are called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "terms",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the ratio."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.231,
            0.846,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ratio of width to height of image C is 30 : 20, and that of image D is 90 : 60."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.281,
            0.835,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In a ratio of the form a : b, we can say that for every ‘a’ units of the first quantity, there are ‘b’ units of the second quantity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.336,
            0.848,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, in image A, we can say that for every 60 mm of width, there are 40 mm of height."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.373,
            0.848,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can say that the ratios of width to height of images A, C, and D are proportional because the terms of these ratios change by the same factor. Let us see how."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.434,
            0.591,
            0.455
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm { I m a g e ~ A } = 6 0 : 4 0"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.46,
            0.561,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Multiplying both the terms by "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.432,
            0.483,
            0.566,
            0.511
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_2_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "60 \\times \\frac{1}{2} : 40 \\times \\frac{1}{2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.511,
            0.687,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "which is 30 : 20, the ratio of width to height in image C."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.542,
            0.847,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ By what factor should we multiply the ratio 60 : 40 (image A) to get 90 : 60 (image D)?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.585,
            0.848,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A more systematic way to compare whether the ratios are proportional is to reduce them to their "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "simplest form",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and see if these simplest forms are the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.657,
            0.605,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.3 Ratios in their Simplest Form"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.691,
            0.847,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can reduce ratios to their simplest form by dividing the terms by their HCF."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.728,
            0.848,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In image A, the terms are 60 and 40. What is the HCF of 60 and 40? It is 20. Dividing the terms by 20, we get the ratio of image A to be 3 : 2 in its simplest form."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.783,
            0.849,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ratio of image D is 90 : 60. Dividing both terms by 30 (HCF of 90 and 60), we get the simplest form to be 3 : 2. So the ratios of images A and D are proportional as well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.84,
            0.716,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is the simplest form of the ratios of images B and E?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.859,
            0.731,
            0.878
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ratio of image B is 40 : 20; in its simplest form, it is 2 : 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.878,
            0.732,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ratio of image E is 60 : 60; in its simplest form, it is 1 : 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.872,
            0.914,
            0.92,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "161"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
